CLASS:: PulsarBlip
SUMMARY:: Polyphonic pulsar generator built on the BlipSync kernel
CATEGORIES:: UGens>Generators>Granular
RELATED:: Classes/BlipSync, Classes/PhaseLock

DESCRIPTION::

Each trigger starts a GRAIN on one of STRONG::numVoices:: internal voices. A
grain is a band-limited blip train at STRONG::freq::, decaying in amplitude over
STRONG::decay:: and in brightness over STRONG::damp::. Because every voice has
its own phase and its own copy of every parameter, grains OVERLAP instead of
replacing one another, and each keeps the settings it was born with.

That is pulsar synthesis in its usual terms:

TELETYPE::
    emission rate   the trigger            pitch above ~20 Hz, rhythm below
    pulsaret freq   freq                   a FORMANT, not the pitch
    pulsaret dur    decay                  blips per grain ~= decay * freq
    pulsaret shape  maxfreq, tilt, damp, rotate
    duty cycle      decay * emission rate  greater than 1 means grains overlap
::

The two rates are independent, which is the whole point of the model: sweep the
emission rate from 4 Hz to 200 Hz with STRONG::freq:: fixed and the pitch climbs
thirty-fold through the rhythm/pitch boundary while the formant does not move
(measured spectral centroid 1828 / 1825 / 1851 / 2146 Hz across that sweep, the
last rising only because the grains have started to overlap).

STRONG::Two things this does that separate oscillators cannot::

Every parameter is SNAPSHOT when a grain is born, so modulating STRONG::freq::
or STRONG::rotate:: scatters the cloud instead of bending all of it together.
Feed them noise at audio rate and each grain takes its own value.

The onset is BAND-MATCHED. A grain would otherwise start with its amplitude
stepping 0 to 1 at the exact instant the blip is at its peak, which is a
discontinuity and therefore broadband. Instead the phase starts a little early
and a raised cosine of the same length brings the amplitude up, so the impulse
arrives precisely as the window reaches 1. The length is two periods of
STRONG::maxfreq::, the only timescale the waveform has. Measured against an 8x
oversampled render, this is code::-76.1 dB:: against code::-24.8 dB:: for the
same texture built from a round-robin of link::Classes/BlipSync:: voices.

NOTE::
The window is a duration, never a sample count. Rounding it to samples makes the
grain's onset time depend on the sample rate, which shifts every grain by a few
microseconds -- and for impulses this narrow, a few microseconds is a large
error. In the like-for-like test it measured -15.3 dB against -61.4 dB, worse
than the round-robin it was meant to beat.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: trig
Emission trigger; each rising crossing of zero starts a grain. Under
code::trigMode: 1:: this is a 0..1 phase ramp instead -- a link::Classes/Phasor::,
a link::Classes/PhaseLock:: output -- and a grain starts on every wrap.

ARGUMENT:: freq
Pulsaret frequency: the blip rate INSIDE a grain, which is a formant, not the
pitch. Snapshot per grain.

ARGUMENT:: decay
Grain duration, seconds to fall 60 dB. Snapshot per grain. Blips per grain is
about code::decay * freq::, so code::decay <= 1/freq:: gives a single blip and
there is no separate mode for it.

ARGUMENT:: damp
Seconds for the grain's spectrum to fall from STRONG::tilt:: to a sine. Short is
a click, long stays bright. Snapshot per grain.

ARGUMENT:: bend
Pitch multiplier at the onset, falling to STRONG::freq:: over STRONG::damp::.
Snapshot per grain.

ARGUMENT:: tilt
Spectral slope at the onset in dB per kHz. Snapshot per grain.

ARGUMENT:: rotate
Impulse asymmetry in cycles; 0.25 is the Hilbert transform. Snapshot per grain.

ARGUMENT:: maxfreq
Band limit in Hz. LIVE, not snapshot: it is the anti-aliasing limit and has to
follow the sample rate rather than the grain. It also sets the impulse width
(about code::0.6/maxfreq::) and the length of the onset window.

ARGUMENT:: minfreq
Bottom of the harmonic band. LIVE.

ARGUMENT:: numVoices
How many grains may sound at once, up to 64. When they are all busy the quietest
is stolen. Inactive voices cost nothing, and a voice whose shaping envelopes have
finished stops rebuilding its band, so a long ring-out is cheap. INIT RATE.

ARGUMENT:: trigMode
0: STRONG::trig:: is a trigger. 1: it is a 0..1 phase ramp and a grain starts on
the wrap. INIT RATE.

EXAMPLES::

Complete patches are in code::examples/pulsar.scd:: in the source tree.

CODE::
s.boot;

// the pulsar demonstration: the emission rate sweeps from rhythm into pitch
// while the formant stays exactly where it is
(
{ PulsarBlip.ar(Impulse.ar(XLine.ar(4, 200, 12)), 600, 0.035, 0.02,
                numVoices: 16) * 0.03 ! 2 }.play
)

// a cloud: irregular emission, and freq is snapshot per grain, so every grain
// has its own pitch
(
{
    PulsarBlip.ar(Dust.ar(40), LFNoise0.ar(200).exprange(120, 1600), 0.25, 0.04,
                  rotate: LFNoise0.ar(200).range(0, 0.3), numVoices: 24) * 0.02 ! 2
}.play
)
::

STRONG::Notes::

LIST::
## Normalisation is raw, so a grain's fundamental sits at 1.0 and its peak is the
harmonic count; overlapping grains add. Scale with code::mul::, and remember the
worst case is all voices sounding at once.
## code::decay:: is clamped to a minimum of 0.5 ms, because a voice that cannot
decay can never be freed.
## Grains are independent of each other, so nothing here needs the pitch to be an
integer multiple of the emission rate -- that constraint belongs to
link::Classes/BlipSync::'s sync, where one phase is being reset rather than a new
voice started.
## A SUSTAINED tone is one grain that never ends: code::decay: 1e6, damp: 0:: and
a single trigger gives a plain blip train matching link::Classes/BlipSync:: to
within the phase offset of the onset window. But STRONG::freq:: is snapshot, so
that tone cannot be bent afterwards -- for a sustained oscillator you can play,
sweep and sync, use link::Classes/BlipSync::. These are two instruments, not
one, and trying to make either do both jobs is what made STRONG::freq:: confusing
in the first place.
::
